Title:
open·kritt — Orchestrate AI agents to find real vulnerabilities in code

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4–8 sentences max):
- open·kritt is an open-source, self-hosted platform for orchestrating multiple AI agents to perform focused security research on codebases and dependencies.  
- It addresses the common failure mode of “scan the whole repo with one prompt” by breaking research into small, well-defined workflow steps executed in parallel and then consolidating results.  
- The system builds reusable workflows (prompt chains), runs scans against local/remote repos using providers like Codex/Claude (and via OpenAI/Anthropic/OpenRouter), and supports post-processing scripts to validate findings, generate PoCs, and produce reports.  
- It emphasizes de-duplication, consistent finding schemas, and configurable severity ranking to prioritize actionable issues.  
- It’s primarily useful for security researchers, bug bounty hunters, and security-minded developers who want control over prompts, model providers, and infrastructure.  
- Operationally, it runs tool-enabled agents as root in disposable Docker job containers with writable repo copies and internet access, and includes a documented threat model due to the risks of scanning untrusted code.

Technical Focus:
- AI-agent orchestration and parallelized workflow execution
- Prompt-chaining “playbooks” for vulnerability research
- Finding normalization: schema, de-duplication, severity ranking
- Post-script validation/enrichment (PoC building, report generation)
- Self-hosted Docker-based execution environment and threat modeling
- Multi-provider LLM integration (Codex, Claude, OpenAI/Anthropic/OpenRouter)

Use Cases:
- Build repeatable AI-assisted bug bounty workflows for specific vulnerability classes
- Triage and de-duplicate AI-generated findings across large repos/monorepos
- Validate suspected issues with automated post-scripts (tests, builds, PoCs)
- Run controlled internal security reviews with bring-your-own-model credentials
- Generate structured vulnerability reports from consolidated agent output

Keywords:
open-kritt, AI agents, agent orchestration, vulnerability research, bug bounty, workflow builder, prompt chaining, LLM, Codex, Claude Code, OpenAI API, Anthropic API, OpenRouter, de-duplication, severity ranking, finding schema, PoC generation, Docker Compose, disposable containers, threat model